+## FastBi1K5 API (magnet — CAEN ELS FAST-PS 1K5, raw TCP 192.168.0.10:10001)
+
+For the session-template safeties cell. Class `drivers.FastBi1K5.FastBi1K5(name, host)`
+— a `qcodes.Instrument` subclass. **Reworked 2026-06-12** (magnet-API
+incident follow-up): `mag` is now a real qcodes Parameter (get + set);
+`get_field()` was removed. Read-truth verdict (CAEN ELS *Command Reference
+Manual, Fast-Bi-1K5 - Fast-PS+*): `GET:I:?` is the **measured** output
+current, averaged (2-stage moving-average filter, ~41 ms lag — ~0.1 mT at
+our slew); `GET:I:SAMPLE:?` is the instantaneous single sample. Neither is
+the commanded set-point; `mag`'s get uses the averaged readback × `T_per_A`.
+
+**Constants (instance attributes, hard-coded in `__init__`, mutable):**
+- `T_per_A = 1/7.3` ≈ 0.13699 T/A (coil constant)
+- `max_field_T = 14.0` T — the magnet limit (ruled 2026-06-12; 13.5
+  before that); `mag()` raises `ValueError` if `|target| > 14.0`.
+  Session caps below it come from the device card / SAFETIES cell.
+- `slew_T_per_s = 1/7.3/60` ≈ 2.283 mT/s ≈ **0.137 T/min (= 1 A/min)** —
+  the ramp rate, fixed here, sent with every setpoint. Not configurable
+  via any method; change the attribute to change the rate. 8 T → 0 takes
+  ~58 min at this rate.
+- `timeout_s = 2` (socket).
+
+**Parameter / methods:**
+- `mag` — qcodes Parameter, unit T. **Get**: `magnet.mag()` returns the
+  measured field in Tesla (averaged current readback × T_per_A);
+  `fp()`-followable as a recorded channel. **Set**: `magnet.mag(target)`
+  ramps, **BLOCKING** until done — sequence unchanged from the old bound
+  method: (1) raises ValueError if `|target| > max_field_T`, or if
+  persistent switch heater is off, or if output is disabled; (2) sends
+  `SET:I:<slew_A_per_s>:<target_A>`; (3) **busy-polls** `REG:STATUS` bit 21
+  (ramping flag) with no sleep between socket queries until clear;
+  (4) sleeps 3 s dwell, returns. Sweepable as before
+  (`s.multimegasweep([magnet.mag], ...)`). **Column-name change**:
+  `full_name` is now `'magnet_mag'` (instrument-bound) — rig-era data
+  (run 80) recorded the spoofed `'mag'`; cross-round analysis must use the
+  right name per round.
+- `status()` — prints a formatted block (field T, current A, voltage V,
+  output enabled, persistent switch, loop mode, update mode). Returns None.
+  `_status()` returns the dict if the templates want values (one query
+  bundle per call).
+- `get_field()` — **REMOVED 2026-06-12**; use `magnet.mag()`.
+- `heater_on()` / `heater_off()` — persistent switch heater; each blocks
+  10 s. **No field-matching logic** — no check that supply current matches
+  persistent current before toggling. Treat as operator-only, with care.
+- No ramp-to-zero helper, no abort/pause method, no persistent-mode
+  capture. **Interrupting a notebook cell mid-`mag()` does not stop the
+  supply ramp** — it keeps ramping to the last setpoint; recovery is
+  calling `mag()` again with a new target.
+
+**Safety notes for the templates:**
+- Field limit enforcement = the single `max_field_T=14.0` check. Templates
+  should impose the session's own (lower) limit before calling `mag()`.
+- Ramp rate is hard-coded sane (1 A/min); don't touch `slew_T_per_s`.
+- `mag()` blocking + 3 s dwell means a fan's B-row step is "free" delay;
+  matches observed ~30 s/B-row overhead in the run-84-era timings.
+
+## SRS_DC205: vti_drivers vs sharpelab_nb
+
+Two distinct drivers, same class name. Both: `output`, `voltage`,
+`voltage_range`, `smart_voltage` params; `ramp_voltage()` method; 115200 baud.
+
+| Aspect | `vti_drivers.SRS_DC205` | `sharpelab_nb.drivers.srs_dc205` |
+|---|---|---|
+| Ramp model | **Rate-based**: `ramp_rate = 5` V/s (mutable attr), 50 points, wait = ΔV/rate/50 | **Fixed-duration**: 100 points × 0.01 s ≈ 1 s + comm regardless of ΔV (effective rate ∝ ΔV — a 10 V move ramps at ~10 V/s) |
+| Post-ramp settle | None (commented out) | **Mandatory `sleep(10)` when ΔV > 0.1 V** |
+| `voltage` set format | `VOLT {}` | `VOLT {:.7f}` |
+| Extras | — | `Ints(0,2)` validator on range; ctor kwargs for ramp tuning |
+
+**Recommendation: use `vti_drivers.SRS_DC205` in the VTI templates.**
+Reasons: (1) the rig notebook was written against it — measure cell 78's
+`dc205_volt = qcodes.Parameter('DC_bias', set_cmd=lambda v: k_tg.ramp_voltage(v))`
+assumes its ramp semantics, and the reference data (runs 79/80) was taken
+with it; (2) rate-based ramping gives a deterministic, gate-safe dV/dt at
+any move size; (3) the sharpelab driver's 10 s settle would silently add
+10 s to every >0.1 V move — fine inside fine sweeps but a timing landmine
+for setup moves and coarse sweeps, and its fixed-duration ramp is *faster*
+than 5 V/s on large moves. They are not interchangeable; templates must
+import explicitly: `from vti_drivers.SRS_DC205 import SRS_DC205`.
+
+## Behavioral deltas vs the rig kernel (accepted, no action)
+
+- `Station._init_logger` writes `log.log` inside the basedir — **basedir
+  must exist before `async_station(...)` is constructed** (same as rig).
+- Sharpelab `_instruments()` snapshots every followed instrument into
+  metadata at sweep start (one extra full snapshot query round per
+  instrument per run). New behavior vs rig; adds a second or two at sweep
+  start, valuable provenance. JSON-serialization of SR830/SR860/K2400
+  snapshots is standard.
+- Vendored `async_station` creates a fresh ThreadPoolExecutor per data
+  point (rig behavior, kept for fidelity). The fork's own
+  `sweep.AsyncStation` reuses one executor and batch-reads SR830 X/P via
+  `SNAP?` — a clean post-session upgrade path, not for tomorrow.
+- `comments` are station-lifetime, not per-run (both sides identical):
+  every `add_comment` since station construction appears in every
+  subsequent run's metadata.
+
+## Shims / actions before tomorrow
+
+1. **Pin kernel Python >= 3.12** — *required, ~5 min.* On dgg-vti uv will
+   likely pick 3.13/3.14 anyway, but the floor allows 3.10 (which also
+   flips qcodes to 0.52.0 per uv.lock). Add `.python-version` (e.g. `3.13`)
+   at repo root, or set `requires-python = ">=3.12"`, before `uv sync` on
+   the rig. Verify with `uv run python -V` then
+   `uv run python -c "import vti_drivers.FastBi1K5"` during preflight.
+2. **Metadata v2 note for analysis** — *no code shim; ~1 line in templates.*
+   Analysis cells must use `sweep.sweep_load.pload`/`load_meta` (reads both
+   schemas) rather than raw `json.load` + singular keys. Old rig data stays
+   readable from our kernel; new data needs plural keys if read raw.
+3. **Session field limit in safeties cell** — *template content, Agent B.*
+   Driver allows up to 13.5 T; encode the session cap (8 T per campaign
+   history, confirm with Qingrui) above any `magnet.mag()` call.
+4. **No shim needed** for sweep API, qcodes drivers, output format, or the
+   DC205 (use the vendored one).
+
